```

---

# 56. REAL-TIME UPDATES

Use SSE/WebSocket/current project mechanism.

Clients reconnecting must not lose durable state.

The database/event store remains source of truth.

Realtime channel is an optimization.

---

# 57. OBSERVABILITY

Add structured metrics:

```text
job_queue_depth
job_claim_latency
job_execution_duration
lease_expirations
worker_count
worker_utilization
outbox_backlog
outbox_failures
webhook_ingest_rate
github_rate_remaining
provider_errors
db_pool_usage
```

Avoid job/repo IDs as metric labels.

---

# 58. DISTRIBUTED TRACING

Propagate trace/correlation context:

```text
job
attempt
worker
runtime
GitHub action
Anthropic call
```

Do not put secrets in spans.

---

# 59. LOGGING

All services should emit structured logs.

Include:

```text
service
instance
job_id where relevant
worker_id
event
```

Do not emit installation tokens or private keys.

---

# 60. BACKPRESSURE

Control plane must survive large bursts.

Apply bounded:

```text
job queue processing
webhook processing
outbox execution
GitHub polling
provider requests
```

Do not spawn a task for every database row without limits.

---

# 61. DEAD-LETTER HANDLING

Repeatedly failing outbox/inbox operations should become inspectable.

Support:

```text
DEAD
```

with operator retry.

Never retry irreversible failures forever.

---

# 62. ADMIN COMMANDS

Add coherent commands:

```bash
zcoder db status
zcoder db migrate

zcoder workers list
zcoder workers drain ...

zcoder repos list
zcoder repos enable ...

zcoder installations list

zcoder queue status

zcoder outbox list
zcoder outbox retry ...

zcoder doctor
```

Support JSON output.

---

# 63. LOCAL MODE

Preserve easy development:

```bash
zcoder run "Fix tests"
```

should continue to work without PostgreSQL when local SQLite mode is selected.

Do not make production architecture destroy local DX.

---

# 64. PRODUCTION MODE

Production configuration should require explicit:

```text
storage=postgresql
web authentication
GitHub App
secure webhook secret
secure secret references
```

Do not silently fall back to SQLite in production mode.

Fail fast.

---

# 65. CONFIG VALIDATION

Provide config validation:

```bash
zcoder config validate
```

Detect:

```text
missing database
invalid GitHub App configuration
insecure webhook
unsupported worker/runtime
conflicting policies
```

---

# 66. SECRET REFERENCES

Use abstraction:

```text
SecretRef
```

Support environment/file initially if necessary.

Design for secret-manager adapters.

Do not persist secret values in PostgreSQL.

---

# 67. WORKFLOW FILE SECURITY

GitHub App permission model must recognize that modifying:

```text
.github/workflows/**
```

is more sensitive than ordinary source edits.

Require explicit permission/policy.

Do not broaden GitHub App permissions globally merely so one repository can edit workflows.

---

# 68. GITHUB APP PER-INSTALLATION POLICY

Different installations may grant different permissions.

Read the actual installation permissions.

Do not assume configured App permissions are always available in every installation context.

---

# 69. REPOSITORY REMOVAL / SUSPENSION

Handle:

```text
installation suspended
installation deleted
repository removed from installation
repository archived
repository renamed/transferred
```

Reconcile local state.

Do not continue mutation jobs with stale authorization.

---

# 70. WEBHOOK INSTALLATION EVENTS

Process relevant installation/repository lifecycle events so fleet state stays accurate.

Do not enable every repository automatically after installation change.

---

# 71. MULTI-REPO JOBS

Do not implement cross-repository mutation until transaction semantics are defined.

For Upgrade-08:

support fleet management across many repos

but each normal engineering job should have one primary repository.

Cross-repo orchestration may be deferred.

---

# 72. FAIR SCHEDULING ACROSS REPOS

One noisy repository must not consume every worker.

Implement configurable concurrency:

```text
global
per installation
per repository
per runtime
```

---

# 73. COST QUOTAS

Support policy scopes:

```text
job
repository
installation
organization
```

At minimum track usage at these scopes.

Hard provider guarantees should remain distinct from ZCoder advisory quotas.

---

# 74. BUDGET CIRCUIT BREAKER

If unexpected cost/rate behavior occurs:

allow system to pause new jobs for affected scope.

Do not terminate unrelated running jobs without explicit policy.

---

# 75. DISASTER RECOVERY

Document backup requirements for PostgreSQL.

Critical recoverable state includes:

```text
jobs
events
approvals
GitHub mappings
outbox
installations metadata
audit
```

Secrets remain outside DB where designed.

---

# 76. BACKUP/RESTORE TEST

Perform at least one integration-level logical backup/restore test if feasible.

Verify restored data preserves:

```text
job IDs
event sequence
PR mappings
worker-independent state
```

Do not claim disaster recovery without testing restoration.

---

# 77. HIGH AVAILABILITY CLAIMS

Do not claim HA simply because multiple API processes can start.

Define:

```text
control-plane horizontal scaling
worker horizontal scaling
database HA
external-provider dependencies
```

PostgreSQL HA itself may remain deployment responsibility.

State this clearly.

---

# 78. GRACEFUL SHUTDOWN

All service processes must:

```text
stop accepting new work
finish/rollback active transaction
release or allow lease expiry
flush safe event/outbox state
close DB pool
```

Do not abandon a claimed job silently.

---

# 79. ZERO-DOWNTIME MIGRATION PREPARATION

Database migrations used during rolling deployments must account for old/new worker overlap where practical.

Prefer expand/migrate/contract patterns for incompatible schema evolution.

Do not require this complexity for every trivial column addition.

---

# 80. SECURITY THREAT MODEL

Create/update:

```text
docs/THREAT-MODEL.md
```

Cover:

```text
malicious repository
malicious PR
prompt injection
GitHub webhook forgery
stolen GitHub App key
database compromise
worker compromise
stale worker
secret exfiltration
cross-tenant data access
workflow-file attack
supply-chain injection
```

---

# 81. P0 SECURITY TESTS

Mandatory tests:

```text
cross-org repository access denied
stale fencing token rejected
invalid webhook rejected
duplicate delivery ignored
suspended installation cannot mutate
secret never serialized to event
untrusted repo denied protected secret
workflow modification policy enforced
```

---

# 82. POSTGRESQL SECURITY

Use application roles with minimum DB privilege.

Do not connect application as PostgreSQL superuser.

Document required privileges.

---

# 83. SQL INJECTION

All queries must be parameterized.

Dynamic ORDER BY/table names must use validated fixed mappings.

Never concatenate user repository/job identifiers into SQL.

---

# 84. DATABASE TENANT TESTS

If organization isolation exists, tests must attempt cross-scope access deliberately.

Do not trust positive tests only.

---

# 85. TEST MATRIX

Upgrade-08 should add substantially more than a handful of tests.

Required categories:

```text
storage contract
PostgreSQL concurrency
migrations
job claims
fencing
leases
worker recovery
scheduler
outbox
inbox
GitHub App auth
installation routing
repo lifecycle
policy
tenant isolation
dashboard API
security
failure recovery
```

---

# 86. MULTI-PROCESS INTEGRATION TEST

Launch at least:

```text
1 PostgreSQL
1 control-plane service
2 worker processes
```

Queue multiple jobs.

Prove:

```text
no duplicate job execution
fair claims
lease recovery
events persisted
```

---

# 87. MULTI-HOST SIMULATION

Processes can run in separate containers to simulate hosts.

Do not call this proof of network-partition tolerance.

But prove there is no reliance on:

```text
shared Python memory
local lock
shared local SQLite
```

---

# 88. WORKER CRASH INTEGRATION TEST

Kill worker A after claim.

Wait for controlled lease expiry.

Worker B must:

```text
reconcile job
verify external runtime state
acquire newer fencing generation
continue safely
```

No duplicate Managed Agent session.

---

# 89. CONTROL-PLANE CRASH TEST

Kill API/scheduler while workers continue.

Restart.

Database state must allow reconciliation.

---

# 90. DATABASE RESTART TEST

Restart PostgreSQL during idle/active operation in an integration scenario.

Verify:

```text
connection recovery
transaction rollback correctness
no duplicate external side effect
```

---

# 91. GITHUB APP FAKE

Maintain deterministic FakeGitHubApp/FakeGitHubProvider for offline tests.

Simulate:

```text
multiple installations
different permissions
token expiry
installation suspension
rate limits
webhooks
repository removal
```

---

# 92. OPTIONAL LIVE GITHUB APP TESTS

Keep:

```text
pytest -m github_live
```

separate.

Use dedicated test installation/repository.

Do not require credentials for normal CI.

---

# 93. OPTIONAL LIVE ANTHROPIC TESTS

Likewise keep Anthropic live tests separate.

Default CI remains deterministic/offline.

---

# 94. CI SERVICES

GitHub Actions should run PostgreSQL integration tests using a service container or equivalent.

Ensure merge-queue-compatible workflows if merge queue is used.

---

# 95. RELEASE GATE EXPANSION

Add mandatory gates:

```text
POSTGRES_STORAGE
MIGRATIONS
